## Execution Order

Due to the massive scope, I propose executing in this order:

| Phase | Group | Files | Rationale |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Phase 1** | A | [learn-firmware.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/.agents/workflows/learn-firmware.md), `learning_prompt.md` | Establishes rules for all subsequent work |
| **Phase 2** | C | `CPU.md` | Answers deepest user questions, standalone doc |
| **Phase 3** | D (partial) | [Clock_System.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/Clock_System.md), [GPIO.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/GPIO.md), [Timers_and_Counters.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/Timers_and_Counters.md) | Core topics that STM32.md references |
| **Phase 4** | B | [STM32.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Controllers/STM32.md) updates | Can now link to the docs created in Phase 2-3 |
| **Phase 5** | C + D | `HAL.md`, [ADC.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/ADC.md), [PWM.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/PWM.md), [Polling_vs_Interrupt_vs_DMA.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Fundamentals/Polling_vs_Interrupt_vs_DMA.md) | Fills remaining fundamentals |
| **Phase 6** | E | [UART.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Protocols/UART.md) | Protocol documentation |
| **Phase 7** | F | [REAL_TIME_EXAMPLES.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/Firmware/MOT-CONTROLLER-CARD/REAL_TIME_EXAMPLES.md) | Enhancement with graphical diagrams |
| **Phase 8** | G | [README.md](file:///c:/Users/oml/Documents/BitBucket/MOT/Docs/README.md), cross-references | Final cleanup |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is a very large body of work (~2500+ lines of new documentation across 12+ files). Each phase will produce reviewable output. I recommend reviewing after Phase 1 (workflow) and then proceeding with content.

